 Fib levels for SMCI.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Trend:</strong> down. Close $30.43 is under the 20-day ($31.93), the 50-day ($36.96) and the 200-day ($32.58).</li><li><strong>Momentum:</strong> RSI 42.9. Weak, not washed out.</li><li><strong>Ceiling:</strong> the 200-day at $32.58, then $33.43 (38.2% of the fall from $45.77 on Oct 7 to $25.81 on Nov 5), then $35.79 (50%). The 52-week high is $51.40 (Jun 2).</li><li><strong>Floor:</strong> $25.81, the Nov 5 low. Under that, the 52-week low at $19.48 (Mar 23).</li><li><strong>Your tested edge:</strong> does not apply. Price is under the 200-day, so this is not an uptrend, whatever the RSI says.</li><li><strong>Plays:</strong> none open or watching on the scorecard.</li><li><strong>Verdict:</strong> the chart supports the downside. A close above $32.58 makes it neutral; a close above $35.79 would support the upside. A close under $25.81 opens the way to $19.48.</li><li><strong>Against management:</strong> the chart disagrees with the sales story and agrees with the margin story. The sales target was raised; price is 41% under its 52-week high.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"what-would-change-this-read\">What would change this read</h3>\n<ul><li>The next report (date not found): watch the top customer's share, gross margin against 12.0%–12.5%, and operating cash flow.</li><li>Any filing that closes the controls weakness, or any charge or settlement naming the company in the export-control case.</li><li>Nvidia on Nov 17 and Dell on Nov 24: what they say about chip supply, memory cost and server pricing sets SMCI's margin.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"confidence-and-sources\">Confidence and sources</h3>\n<p>Solid: results, guidance, customer concentration, debt, related-party and legal notes (company release, prepared remarks, 10-Q), the CFO change and the board resignation (company announcement copy, 8-K). Thin: call question-and-answer detail; no backlog figure; next report date; the name of the prior auditor; the indictment detail comes from o